WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.646
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.646 --> 00:00:03.910
ALLEY RUTZEL: Hi everyone.

00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:05.960
Thank you so much for coming.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.220
I hope you're having
a great time at Next.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.620
My name is Alley Rutzel
and I'm a senior manager

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.030
of user experience at Google.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:16.072
I work on a new enterprise video
meeting experience in Hangouts,

00:00:16.072 --> 00:00:18.030
which are going to be
hearing a little bit more

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.880
about tomorrow in the keynote.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.790
So I'm here today to give
you a behind-the-scenes look

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:27.000
into Google's usability
design philosophy,

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.950
and how you can apply
what you've learned here

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:32.587
at your organization I will also
be happy to answer questions

00:00:32.587 --> 00:00:34.920
at the end of the session,
there will be plenty of time.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.620
I think I timed myself and
this only took 30 minutes.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:40.930
So, I really do want
to hear from you.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:42.810
So if you have
questions, please feel

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.730
free to ask them at the end.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:51.600
So first, what exactly do I
mean when I say usability?

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.880
A leading expert in the
field defines usability

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:57.210
as a quality attribute
that assesses how easy user

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.460
interfaces are to use.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.620
That's pretty
straightforward, but it's also

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.180
an oversimplification.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.010
The word usability
can also refer

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:08.460
to methods we employ for
improving ease of use

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:09.810
during the design process.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:13.420
And I'll talk a little bit
more about that in a minute.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:15.660
But for now, let's
talk about usability,

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.900
and break it down into what
I consider its six quality

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.020
components.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.200
So learnability-- how
easy is it for users

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.720
to accomplish basic
tasks the first time

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.490
they encounter a design?

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:32.490
The more learnable a system
is, the less time a user

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:36.300
takes in order to understand
how to do a specific task

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.760
without having been
previously trained or provided

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:40.770
documentation.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:44.850
But how important is
learnability in a design?

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:46.920
Think about if you're
designing a user interface

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.190
for a kiosk in a mall.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.290
You want people to be
able to go up to it

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:54.707
and accomplish their
tasks really quickly.

00:01:54.707 --> 00:01:56.790
They don't have to learn
how to use the interface.

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.550
You want them to achieve their
goals and get out of the way.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:03.480
So learnability is incredibly
important in that scenario.

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:04.980
But if you're working
on, let's say,

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.490
a really complex enterprise
business system for customer

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:11.520
service management tools.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.250
Then, the chances are that
they're more likely going

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.060
to invest more time in
learning how to use the system

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.440
and maybe there's
training involved.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.760
So really the point is that
learnability in that case

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.874
is still important, but
other components of usability

00:02:26.874 --> 00:02:27.915
may be a higher priority.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:35.122
Efficiency is another
component of usability.

00:02:35.122 --> 00:02:36.580
Once users have
learned the design,

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:40.330
how quickly can they
perform the tasks?

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.580
Efficiency is typically
measured by the number

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.530
of clicks or
keystrokes required,

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.829
or the total time on task.

00:02:46.829 --> 00:02:49.120
But once again, it's important
to understand your users

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.040
and how they like to work.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.030
For example, are they likely to
use the interface infrequently?

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.040
Or are they going to
be habitual users that

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.900
are in it every day--

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:03.990
maybe they're going to learn
hidden shortcuts and controls.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:05.670
Keyboard shortcuts
can be extremely

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:09.110
efficient for the
proficient users.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:13.110
But those that have to use the
interface a little bit less

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:16.837
often, if shortcuts are the
primary interaction tool,

00:03:16.837 --> 00:03:18.420
they're going to be
really slowed down

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.795
by that because
they're not going

00:03:19.795 --> 00:03:21.480
to understand what
those are and they're

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:22.646
going to have to learn them.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.290
Memorability.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:32.620
When users return to the design
after a period of not using it,

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.612
how easily can they
re-establish proficiency?

00:03:35.612 --> 00:03:37.570
Think about how often
users will be coming back

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:39.160
to your product or service.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.530
Obviously we all want them
to come every single day,

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:45.070
but chances are that they're
not going to do that.

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:48.807
Well, some people will,
but most often they won't.

00:03:48.807 --> 00:03:50.640
For example, I have
diamond status on Delta,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.010
but I don't go to the Delta
site every single day.

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:55.897
But when I do, it's
important that I

00:03:55.897 --> 00:03:56.980
know where to find things.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.990
I want them to be in the same
place that they were before.

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.290
So obviously, design
that's complicated

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:03.820
is going to make
it harder for users

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.640
to remember the exact steps
that they took to accomplish

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:10.060
the task, which is why
efficiency and memorability

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.960
actually go hand in hand.

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.092
If the design is more
efficient, then there

00:04:14.092 --> 00:04:15.550
are going to be
less steps for them

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.459
to go through to
accomplish their tasks

00:04:18.459 --> 00:04:20.500
and less things to remember.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.130
This makes it easier for them
to reestablish proficiency, even

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.260
after a long period of not
having used the product.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:32.770
So accessibility.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:34.630
I think this one is
really important.

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:37.330
Can all users,
regardless of ability,

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:41.620
navigate, understand, and
achieve their goals equally,

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:43.690
without barriers?

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:46.660
In the words of Nicholas
Zachas, a friend

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:50.830
and engineer-- former friend and
engineer Yahoo, accessibility

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.312
is not a feature.

00:04:52.312 --> 00:04:53.770
And I think that's
really important

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:56.200
to take into consideration.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.820
This means that
accessibility is not

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:01.610
an optional item on a
list of product functions,

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:03.970
but instead a mandatory
requirement on par

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:04.870
with performance.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:10.870
As such, every product
should be built

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.230
with accessibility in mind
from the very beginning.

00:05:16.050 --> 00:05:20.610
While accessibility focuses
on people with disabilities,

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:23.190
many accessibility requirements
actually improve usability

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:24.480
for everybody.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.710
So accessibility especially
benefits people that have--

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:31.620
that don't have disabilities,
but are in limiting situations.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:36.360
So imagine that time I broke my
hand slamming it in a car door,

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.060
and I wasn't able to
use the device the way

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.100
that I normally would,
and I had to turn

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.910
on accessibility features
to be able to navigate.

00:05:43.910 --> 00:05:46.290
Or even being outside
in the sunlight

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:49.230
with a phone, or a laptop,
where you're limited

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:52.060
by the contrast on the screen.

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:53.910
So, there are a lot
of things that we

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:57.266
call situational
disabilities that we

00:05:57.266 --> 00:05:59.640
need to be thinking about when
we're designing our tools.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.300
Errors.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.980
How many errors do users
make, and how severe

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:09.200
are these errors?

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.200
And how easily can
they recover from them?

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:14.780
Errors are inevitable.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:15.650
They happen.

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:17.234
Unintended actions happen.

00:06:17.234 --> 00:06:19.400
Think about how many times
you're filling in a form.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.250
Maybe you mistype
your email address,

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:24.860
put in the wrong password.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.740
A lot of times, when I'm filling
out my credit card expiration

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.690
date, I put the wrong month in.

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:33.170
Then I get a big error, and I
have to go back and redo it.

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:36.350
But there's a difference between
these types of slips and user

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:38.770
interface problems.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.540
If users continue to click on
a heading that's not actually

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:45.110
clickable, or they try to look
for something in the wrong part

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:47.150
of your navigation
system, then there's

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:51.980
probably something about the
design that we can improve.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:53.690
One action is to take--

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:55.430
one action to take
is to make sure

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:58.190
all of the elements in
your UI are instrumented

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:00.320
for performance
metrics, so that you

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:02.510
can understand what
people are doing

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:04.165
when they use your product.

00:07:04.165 --> 00:07:05.540
And if you don't
know what people

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:08.000
are doing, what
errors they're making,

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.670
you're not going to
be able to fix it.

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:13.850
So, a lot of times we just
look at performance metrics

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:17.450
for things that are
pretty standard.

00:07:17.450 --> 00:07:21.980
But think about the elements
of your UI that you want,

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.089
you might not know that
people are clicking on,

00:07:24.089 --> 00:07:25.130
but they are clicking on.

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:26.880
The non-clickable
things, like that header

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:27.920
that I talked about.

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:31.530
You might not instrument it,
because it's not a click.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.800
But if people think it is,
you want to know that, too.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:39.050
And finally satisfaction.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:41.750
Satisfaction, I
consider is probably

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:43.220
the hardest to measure.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:44.960
Satisfaction is
about how pleasant

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:48.600
is it to use your design.

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:52.470
Often, how often have
you been on a site, when

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.990
all of a sudden, you're
trying to accomplish a task,

00:07:54.990 --> 00:07:57.090
all of a sudden this
satisfaction survey

00:07:57.090 --> 00:08:01.440
pops up, like a weird thing that
kind of just jumps in your face

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:03.572
and interrupts your flow.

00:08:03.572 --> 00:08:05.280
First of all, that's
not very satisfying.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:07.859
But think about it.

00:08:07.859 --> 00:08:09.900
People use that, businesses
use that because they

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:11.880
want to know what you think.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:14.070
So we have to find ways
of asking our users what

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:17.640
they think without
being so annoying.

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:19.320
You can do this in
usability studies.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:21.450
A lot of people will actually
use usability studies

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:24.492
as a way to gather that
qualitative information.

00:08:24.492 --> 00:08:25.200
And that's great.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:28.590
I love usability studies,
we do them all the time.

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:31.170
But sometimes we notice that
participants tend to rate

00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:35.159
an interface highly on post-test
questionnaires even when they

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:37.140
fail to complete the task.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.197
And the reason they
do that is for--

00:08:39.197 --> 00:08:40.030
a couple of reasons.

00:08:40.030 --> 00:08:43.590
One, the kind of
want to impress you.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:45.760
They want, you
know, hey, I'm here.

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:46.890
I can really do this.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:47.940
It's great.

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:50.820
You know, they don't want to
say anything negative to you.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.430
Sometimes you're paying them
to participate in the study,

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.450
so they feel like they
have to please you.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:08:59.550
But the worst one, the
one that makes me cringe,

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:01.740
is that they often
blame themselves.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:05.970
That it wasn't the UI's problem,
it was my fault that I failed.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:11.070
And so they don't want to show
that that was their fault.

00:09:11.070 --> 00:09:12.960
They want to say,
everything was great.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:14.100
It was wonderful.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.740
It's not you it's me.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:20.730
So unless they're carefully
worded, and another thing we

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:21.780
use is surveys.

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:23.460
But unless they are
carefully worded,

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:27.180
surveys can also result in
what we call acquiescence bias.

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:29.070
The fact that people
are more likely to agree

00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:32.580
with a statement than
disagree with a statement.

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:35.147
So those satisfaction
surveys or those 4C surveys,

00:09:35.147 --> 00:09:37.230
or whatever, you'll notice
that they are carefully

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:40.350
crafted so that they're
not biased or leading.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:42.930
So it's hard, but
it's not impossible.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.480
Good user researchers know
the proper methodologies

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:47.610
to use for measuring
satisfaction,

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:51.820
and I'll talk a little bit about
how we do that in a minute.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:54.810
But now how we think
about usability,

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:57.510
the components of
which are learnability,

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:02.490
efficiency, memorability,
accessibility, errors,

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:05.460
and satisfaction, let me
share a little bit more

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:07.650
about our usability
philosophy at Google.

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:13.290
It's pretty simple--
focus on the user,

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:15.690
and all else will follow.

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:16.900
So how do we do that?

00:10:16.900 --> 00:10:19.020
How do we focus on the user?

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:22.500
We do it by involving the
user in every phase of product

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:23.579
development.

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:30.330
When we think about the
phases of product development,

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:33.440
we typically think of a
temporal progression from idea

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:34.370
to launch.

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.050
Some of you may have
seen this framework

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:39.590
before, it's often referred
to as the double diamond.

00:10:39.590 --> 00:10:42.110
During the discover
and planning phases,

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:46.160
we're trying to figure out what
the right thing is to build.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:49.490
This phase is divergent
and exploratory.

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:52.040
It's a search for new
questions, before we

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:54.470
start to then synthesize
knowledge into an insight.

00:10:54.470 --> 00:10:57.930
That's why we go wide
before we start to narrow.

00:10:57.930 --> 00:11:00.510
In the remaining phases
of conceptual design,

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:04.250
detailed design,
and build, we're

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:06.770
focused on designing the right--

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:08.600
designing the thing right.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:12.380
We take the insights that we
learned in the first phase,

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:14.030
and we explore
several solutions.

00:11:14.030 --> 00:11:17.150
And then we narrow it down,
and craft the working solution

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:19.550
that we plan to bring to market.

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:22.976
This linear path helps us feel
like we're making progress.

00:11:22.976 --> 00:11:24.350
But as you probably
already know,

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.370
if you've ever done
product development,

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:29.100
this is not how
things typically go.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:33.620
This is what the process
looks like for real.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:35.960
Yeah, this is more like it.

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:39.270
The reality is the
process is really messy.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:42.470
You have an idea, you try
it out, you argue about it,

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:43.220
you mock it up.

00:11:43.220 --> 00:11:45.480
Maybe you test it
out on a few users.

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:47.270
You realize that
they don't like it,

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:51.644
or there's something wrong with
it, so you try something else.

00:11:51.644 --> 00:11:53.060
Then, maybe you
realize that there

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:54.927
are technical difficulties,
and the back end

00:11:54.927 --> 00:11:57.260
can't support what you're
doing and makes it unfeasible.

00:11:57.260 --> 00:11:59.090
So you have to loop back
somewhere near the beginning

00:11:59.090 --> 00:11:59.900
and start again.

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:03.200
Somewhere in there
there's coffee and beer.

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:05.940
But your goals are
still the same.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:08.570
You want to design
the right thing,

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:09.810
and design the thing right.

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:19.090
In other words, you want
it to address user needs,

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:21.730
and you want it to be usable.

00:12:21.730 --> 00:12:23.560
So let's dive into
these phases and see

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:26.650
how you can keep the user at the
center of this messy process,

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:28.190
how you can focus on the user.

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:33.800
So during the
discovery phase, you

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:37.260
wanted to uncover opportunities
for design improvement.

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:39.050
If you have an existing product.

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:41.810
If you have a brand new product
that you're just working on,

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:44.450
you also want to start
looking at the market

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:47.030
and understanding the needs
that are out there that

00:12:47.030 --> 00:12:50.420
aren't being fulfilled.

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:52.940
This phase should be a
really close partnership

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:56.660
between UX, engineering,
and product management.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:13:00.320
But you should also involve
operations, support, marketing,

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:01.370
sales.

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:03.920
Any other specialists
in the organization

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:07.760
that talk to your users, so that
you can gather as much data as

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:10.400
possible about them.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:14.540
The goal here is to
understand user needs,

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:16.847
understand who your users
are, and how they currently

00:13:16.847 --> 00:13:17.555
use your product.

00:13:20.220 --> 00:13:23.000
Quantitative research like
metrics and log analysis

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:25.220
really helps you
understand what of people

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:27.380
are doing, what
users are doing when

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:29.350
they're using your product.

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:32.930
But qualitative research, such
as ethnographic studies, which

00:13:32.930 --> 00:13:36.132
is going out into the field
and actually observing users

00:13:36.132 --> 00:13:37.340
in their natural environment.

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:39.360
Sounds like Dian
Fossey, but it is.

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:41.870
It's about going out there and
meeting them where they work,

00:13:41.870 --> 00:13:44.750
seeing the environment
that they work in.

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:48.140
That helps you uncover
the why behind the what.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:50.195
And sometimes the
why can surprise you.

00:13:50.195 --> 00:13:52.070
So I'm going take a
little aside and tell you

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:55.880
a funny story that my
husband told me long ago.

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:58.460
He's a-- my husband's
a fiber optic engineer.

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:01.430
And there was a time when
a customer complained

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:05.150
about temporary outages
that were happening

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:07.050
around the same time each day.

00:14:07.050 --> 00:14:09.420
And so he would look at
the data, and he would see,

00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:11.970
yeah, there's a brief
outage, and it seems

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:13.790
to happen in the afternoon.

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:15.950
And they ran tests
and diagnostics

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:19.191
and they couldn't really figure
out why this was happening.

00:14:19.191 --> 00:14:20.690
He sent technicians
out to the field

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:22.550
to look for those
usual suspects.

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:26.060
Apparently squirrels like
to gnaw on the wires,

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:27.630
and that can cause outages.

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:30.213
So they looked for little frays
and everything like that, they

00:14:30.213 --> 00:14:32.370
couldn't find anything.

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:33.950
So he was becoming
really frustrated,

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:35.919
because he couldn't
figure out what was wrong.

00:14:35.919 --> 00:14:38.210
So what he decided to do is
he sent the technician over

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:41.330
to the customer's site one day,
and he went out in the truck

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.142
with another technician,
and they just kind of went

00:14:44.142 --> 00:14:46.100
around the area where
the outage was happening.

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:49.732
It's hard to pinpoint
exactly where it is.

00:14:49.732 --> 00:14:50.690
And something happened.

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:53.300
He noticed a little
boy, about 10 years old,

00:14:53.300 --> 00:14:54.740
walking home from school.

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:57.170
He had a stick in his hand.

00:14:57.170 --> 00:15:00.860
And as they were walking, as
he was walking down the street,

00:15:00.860 --> 00:15:02.570
he approaches what's
called a down guy.

00:15:02.570 --> 00:15:04.910
And that's-- when you think
about a telephone pole,

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:06.550
and the way it
anchors to the ground.

00:15:06.550 --> 00:15:11.240
That angular wire,
that's called a down guy.

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:13.880
He would take that stick as
he approached it and he would

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:16.130
give it a good thwack.

00:15:16.130 --> 00:15:20.630
And what was happening was
that the vibration of the wire

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:23.600
was interrupting the light
path of the optical cable.

00:15:23.600 --> 00:15:28.280
And so while it was vibrating
the outage happened, and then

00:15:28.280 --> 00:15:31.820
it would stabilize and
the outage would stop.

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:36.380
So sure enough, moments after
the kid thwacks that line,

00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:37.700
my husband gets a call.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:39.870
Customer has an outage.

00:15:39.870 --> 00:15:43.550
So he went to the
kid, and he was like,

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:45.950
do you do this every day?

00:15:45.950 --> 00:15:48.230
The kid is like,
yeah, I like the sound

00:15:48.230 --> 00:15:49.430
it makes when I hit it.

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:51.900
Because it does, it
makes this weird hum.

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:55.490
And he's like, oh my
god, we figured it out.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:57.980
And so my point is that
there are some problems

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:00.650
that you'll never really
understand until you actually

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:05.940
go out in the field and
observe what people are doing.

00:16:05.940 --> 00:16:09.710
So let me give you an example
of what I've experienced

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:13.910
on my own product in this area.

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:17.120
So in 2013, Hangouts
was a part of G+.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:18.620
Through logs
analysis, we noticed

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:20.120
that it was becoming
increasingly

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:22.460
popular among business users.

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:24.330
So we reached out to
them to find out more.

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:26.270
We wanted to know why.

00:16:26.270 --> 00:16:28.430
We conducted diary
studies and field research

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:31.160
with local businesses, and
based on what they told us

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:33.350
and what we observed,
we were able to uncover

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:35.300
some interesting
insights that lead

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:39.474
to opportunities to improve
the experience for them.

00:16:39.474 --> 00:16:41.390
One thing we saw was
that there were basically

00:16:41.390 --> 00:16:45.530
four types of meetings
where they used Hangouts.

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:46.804
The first is ad hoc.

00:16:46.804 --> 00:16:48.470
These are your typical
pick up meetings.

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:50.630
These are the ones like, hey,
I have a really quick question.

00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:52.850
Do you want to jump on
a Hangout and answer it?

00:16:52.850 --> 00:16:56.344
Let's go into this quick room,
let me show you something.

00:16:56.344 --> 00:16:58.760
Round tables are what we think
of as the more traditional,

00:16:58.760 --> 00:16:59.540
typical meetings.

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:01.873
They are usually scheduled,
sometimes they're recurring.

00:17:01.873 --> 00:17:05.030
Think about your team meetings,
or where you go into--

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:07.790
review something
or solve a problem.

00:17:07.790 --> 00:17:10.160
Structured engagements are
more like sales presentations

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:10.940
or trainings.

00:17:10.940 --> 00:17:12.800
This is where there's
usually one speaker

00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:15.619
and he's presenting to a group.

00:17:15.619 --> 00:17:19.670
And then spotlights, which
are generally larger meetings.

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:22.099
They're more like all-hands
and earning reports,

00:17:22.099 --> 00:17:23.811
where you have one to many.

00:17:23.811 --> 00:17:26.060
They're often broadcast to
a large audience that might

00:17:26.060 --> 00:17:28.339
be remote or watching online.

00:17:28.339 --> 00:17:32.010
Our customers are using Hangouts
on Air for things like that.

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:36.140
So once we got insight into why
and how Hangouts customers were

00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:38.060
using the product, we
were able to conceive

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:40.940
of a new enterprised, focused
take on video meetings that

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:42.420
could better meet their needs.

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:48.640
So the second phase
of the design process

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:51.790
is-- the product development
process is planning.

00:17:51.790 --> 00:17:54.130
This is about
clarifying and outlining

00:17:54.130 --> 00:17:55.500
nuances of the problem.

00:17:58.129 --> 00:18:00.170
The goals here are about
taking what you learned,

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:04.040
and deciding on the
right thing to build.

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:06.500
Some methods that you can
use to help you decide

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:09.380
are just prioritizing
the user problems,

00:18:09.380 --> 00:18:11.900
or creating use
cases and scenarios

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:14.720
to help you understand when
somebody does something.

00:18:14.720 --> 00:18:16.550
What are the scenarios
they're doing it in,

00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:19.400
and what are they
trying to accomplish?

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:21.170
One thing that we
also do on my team

00:18:21.170 --> 00:18:23.960
is what we call a strategy
brief, or a one pager.

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:25.549
And this is before the PRD.

00:18:25.549 --> 00:18:27.590
This is when your first
sitting down and thinking

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:29.810
about the problem.

00:18:29.810 --> 00:18:31.670
It doesn't get into
detail, but instead it

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:35.090
aligns the team on
the high level goals,

00:18:35.090 --> 00:18:36.620
helps you identify
and narrow down

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:39.140
who it is that you're
trying to-- who is your user

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:42.020
and who it is that
you're trying to reach.

00:18:42.020 --> 00:18:44.540
It also defines what
success looks like.

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:46.640
Because without
that North Star, you

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:50.180
can often like your
UX team, your PM team,

00:18:50.180 --> 00:18:52.932
your engineers might all have
different and goals in mind,

00:18:52.932 --> 00:18:54.140
and you might not be aligned.

00:18:54.140 --> 00:18:56.210
So we found a
one-pager, very simple,

00:18:56.210 --> 00:18:59.510
sit down and talk about
these things at a high level

00:18:59.510 --> 00:19:02.750
and make sure you're
on the same page.

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:05.530
In the- and the last method
here is experience mapping,

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:06.970
and I'm going to
talk about that.

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:08.690
In my last example,
I mentioned that we

00:19:08.690 --> 00:19:10.520
decided to focus on
the solving the user

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.250
needs of the common
roundtable meeting type

00:19:13.250 --> 00:19:15.590
first, because that was
something that we felt

00:19:15.590 --> 00:19:18.440
most of our users were doing.

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:21.230
But we didn't understand
what exactly they needed.

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:22.730
So to figure that
out, we decided

00:19:22.730 --> 00:19:26.000
to create an experience
map of a typical meeting.

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:29.627
So what is an experience map?

00:19:29.627 --> 00:19:30.710
This is pretty simplified.

00:19:30.710 --> 00:19:33.170
But an experience map
is a strategic process

00:19:33.170 --> 00:19:36.740
of capturing and communicating
interfaces or interactions

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:39.390
from a user's perspective.

00:19:39.390 --> 00:19:41.750
In this example, I'm showing
a really simplified version

00:19:41.750 --> 00:19:43.500
of the experience map
that we created when

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:46.210
developing our new product.

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:48.080
But what we structured
the map to do

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:50.126
is look at-- when you
think about meetings,

00:19:50.126 --> 00:19:51.750
there are different
stages of meetings.

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:54.260
There's the before the
meeting, during the meeting,

00:19:54.260 --> 00:19:55.910
and after the meeting.

00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:58.984
And on the y-axis we have
what are users doing.

00:19:58.984 --> 00:20:00.650
What are the tasks
that they're actually

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:02.990
doing during those phases?

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:06.390
What are they thinking while
they're doing those things?

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:10.490
How are they feeling
during those actions?

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:12.310
And then, what are
those opportunities

00:20:12.310 --> 00:20:15.287
that the product can--

00:20:15.287 --> 00:20:17.870
what do we see as opportunities
for solving some of those user

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:19.700
needs?

00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:21.350
So, for example,
in the before phase

00:20:21.350 --> 00:20:23.840
we found out that
business users were often

00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:26.120
needing to take meetings
when they were on the road.

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:28.070
So, as you know,
internet connections

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:29.540
can be pretty spotty.

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:31.430
So under Opportunities
we decided

00:20:31.430 --> 00:20:34.034
to put, provide dial-in
access to meetings,

00:20:34.034 --> 00:20:35.450
so that they could
use their phone

00:20:35.450 --> 00:20:38.030
rather than have
to rely on video.

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:41.211
So you can also add a section
for gaps, that's pretty common.

00:20:41.211 --> 00:20:42.710
So you can look at
what the gaps are

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:45.080
in your product
versus the market.

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:47.480
But this is a really
helpful process

00:20:47.480 --> 00:20:51.950
for just really laying out the
landscape of what your user is

00:20:51.950 --> 00:20:55.610
doing and around your product.

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:58.580
Since meetings are spatial
as well as temporal,

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:01.070
we also have to consider a
variety of physical factors

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:02.529
when creating our map.

00:21:02.529 --> 00:21:04.070
For example, before
a meeting begins,

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:05.660
you probably need
to find the room.

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:07.640
So what is the way--finding
system look like?

00:21:07.640 --> 00:21:10.310
How confused are you going
to be about getting there?

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:13.820
How do you know how long it's
going to take you to get there?

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:15.420
And then when
you're in the room,

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:18.039
you need to use a remote to
actually start the meeting

00:21:18.039 --> 00:21:19.580
and maybe control
some of the things,

00:21:19.580 --> 00:21:22.066
like zooming and muting.

00:21:22.066 --> 00:21:23.690
So there are a lot
of physical elements

00:21:23.690 --> 00:21:25.670
that we had to
think about as well.

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:28.280
A lot of times the remote
goes missing in those rooms,

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:30.616
and then what do you do then?

00:21:30.616 --> 00:21:32.990
So the activity of mapping an
experience like this really

00:21:32.990 --> 00:21:36.710
helps organizations identify
strategic opportunities,

00:21:36.710 --> 00:21:39.140
customer pain
points, and helps us

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:41.425
generate innovative products.

00:21:41.425 --> 00:21:42.800
It also helps you
decide on where

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:45.600
to focus your energy before
moving to the conceptual design

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:46.100
phase.

00:21:50.890 --> 00:21:53.020
So conceptual
design is where you

00:21:53.020 --> 00:21:55.470
create the most apt
solution for the problem.

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:58.561
It's where you-- you're entering
this phase when you understand

00:21:58.561 --> 00:22:01.060
what the problem is, but you
don't know yet how to solve it.

00:22:03.679 --> 00:22:06.220
So from talking to our users
and creating the experience map,

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:07.750
we discovered that
joining a video meeting

00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:10.090
can be a pretty painful
process, but we didn't really

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:12.130
know exactly how to solve that.

00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:14.020
In this phase, our
goal is creativity.

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:16.170
We really want to
generate a lot of ideas.

00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:18.460
No ideas are stupid
in this phase.

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:21.550
And then we start testing
and iterating on them.

00:22:21.550 --> 00:22:25.540
We want to design
the thing right.

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:27.820
A great way to involve
the user in this phase

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:31.390
is to do rapid iterative
prototyping and quick usability

00:22:31.390 --> 00:22:32.260
testing.

00:22:32.260 --> 00:22:33.800
It doesn't have to
be on end users.

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:35.110
It can be with
people on your team,

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:36.610
it can be with the
sales department,

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:39.040
it can be with anybody nearby.

00:22:39.040 --> 00:22:41.680
You really just want to
get ideas out there and try

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:43.660
and validate them as
quickly as possible

00:22:43.660 --> 00:22:46.510
as to which is the one that
you want to move forward with.

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:48.900
My advice here is to
stay low fidelity.

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:50.470
And I'm talking sketches.

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:51.310
Wireframes.

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:53.020
Rough user flows.

00:22:53.020 --> 00:22:56.320
This is about the idea,
not the execution.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:59.110
The lower the fidelity,
the better, actually.

00:22:59.110 --> 00:23:01.630
Even when testing with
users, paper prototypes

00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:03.370
are really helpful.

00:23:03.370 --> 00:23:06.550
We've learned that as the
fidelity of the wireframes

00:23:06.550 --> 00:23:10.120
and prototypes goes up,
the quality of the feedback

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:12.940
actually can go down, because
people are hesitant to give you

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:15.700
negative feedback when they
think that you've actually

00:23:15.700 --> 00:23:17.360
put a lot of effort into it.

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:19.950
So sometimes, even if we
work in high fidelity,

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:21.700
we will dumb it down.

00:23:21.700 --> 00:23:24.309
We'll make it a little
bit less polished,

00:23:24.309 --> 00:23:25.850
so when we put it
in front of people,

00:23:25.850 --> 00:23:28.016
they feel a little bit more
comfortable in providing

00:23:28.016 --> 00:23:29.050
feedback on the product.

00:23:33.480 --> 00:23:36.170
So here's an example of what
we showed users early on.

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:37.670
As I mentioned for
video meetings,

00:23:37.670 --> 00:23:40.620
it's critical that attendees
can join quickly and easily.

00:23:40.620 --> 00:23:42.890
So we shared our ideas
for several different ways

00:23:42.890 --> 00:23:45.300
that they could
set up a meeting.

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:47.750
So our options to
them were, hey, you

00:23:47.750 --> 00:23:49.490
can have it be totally
open so anybody

00:23:49.490 --> 00:23:51.980
with a link to the
meeting can join.

00:23:51.980 --> 00:23:54.420
If someone is not invited or
outside the company's domain,

00:23:54.420 --> 00:23:57.470
you can require them
to knock to get in.

00:23:57.470 --> 00:23:59.850
Or you could choose to
completely lock it down,

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:02.600
so it's only open to people
who are on the calendar.

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:05.480
An invitation, no exceptions.

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:07.340
So we tested these
concepts, and people just

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:09.500
couldn't grasp all
the different options

00:24:09.500 --> 00:24:11.160
and why they had
to make a choice.

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:12.535
How do I know
which one is right?

00:24:12.535 --> 00:24:13.490
What if I'm wrong?

00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:16.120
Why shouldn't I be able to
set it up one way and change

00:24:16.120 --> 00:24:17.210
and the next time?

00:24:17.210 --> 00:24:19.670
So we wrangled with
several text changes

00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:21.900
to try and make
everything more clear.

00:24:21.900 --> 00:24:23.840
But ultimately, we
decided to just come up

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:26.960
with a simple default, where if
you're not explicitly invited

00:24:26.960 --> 00:24:30.020
to the meeting, you
can knock to get in.

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:32.930
We found that users expect
us to do the right thing.

00:24:32.930 --> 00:24:34.670
And this was a
great moment when we

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:37.220
realized usability research
can influence the product

00:24:37.220 --> 00:24:38.190
direction.

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:46.090
The detailed design
phase is where

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:48.190
you spec the complete
end-to-end solution.

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:51.790
So once we knew conceptually
where we wanted to go,

00:24:51.790 --> 00:24:53.785
we entered this phase to
nail down the details.

00:24:57.050 --> 00:24:59.060
This is about getting
the details right,

00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:02.930
so your final product is
delightful and easy to use.

00:25:02.930 --> 00:25:04.850
It's about holding a
high bar for quality,

00:25:04.850 --> 00:25:07.220
and about making sure that
experience is consistent,

00:25:07.220 --> 00:25:09.740
so users have to
relearn something

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:12.200
they maybe knew how to
do or should already

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:13.850
be familiar with.

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:15.710
An example that I
can pull from Google

00:25:15.710 --> 00:25:18.840
is when you think about how
you share something in Docs,

00:25:18.840 --> 00:25:21.620
Sheets, or Slides, you
know where that is,

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:23.430
and it always
behaves the same way.

00:25:23.430 --> 00:25:25.580
So why reinvent the
wheel, especially if it's

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:28.550
part of a suite of products?

00:25:28.550 --> 00:25:30.980
This is where the
designers on your team

00:25:30.980 --> 00:25:33.920
are doing the visual polish
and the red lining-- or what

00:25:33.920 --> 00:25:36.380
we call speccing out,
down to the pixel,

00:25:36.380 --> 00:25:39.860
of how the UI should look
and behave for your engineers

00:25:39.860 --> 00:25:41.169
to build.

00:25:41.169 --> 00:25:42.710
This is also a phase
where you really

00:25:42.710 --> 00:25:45.380
want to make sure your
accessibility spec is

00:25:45.380 --> 00:25:48.230
up to date to determine how
screen readers are going to be

00:25:48.230 --> 00:25:51.380
able to interpret your pages.

00:25:51.380 --> 00:25:54.080
For our new video meeting
product, during this phase,

00:25:54.080 --> 00:25:56.240
we also did an
extensive audit of what

00:25:56.240 --> 00:25:57.572
we call critical user journeys.

00:25:57.572 --> 00:25:59.030
And that's the last
method on here.

00:25:59.030 --> 00:26:00.830
And I'll talk about
those right now.

00:26:04.500 --> 00:26:08.450
So users don't think in
terms of single features.

00:26:08.450 --> 00:26:11.270
They think in terms of
accomplishing tasks.

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:13.970
Tasks often involve
multiple features, products,

00:26:13.970 --> 00:26:15.800
or product areas.

00:26:15.800 --> 00:26:19.430
Thus they journey through the
hardware and the software.

00:26:19.430 --> 00:26:21.650
Simply put, a user
journey is a task

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:25.430
that a user wants to perform,
and a critical user journey

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:28.960
is the really important journey
that you have to get right.

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:32.180
At Google, we identify two
types of critical user journeys.

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:35.210
Toothbrush journeys are what
we call the common tasks

00:26:35.210 --> 00:26:37.460
that people perform often.

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:40.940
And pivotal journeys are
less frequent, but also

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:42.770
important tasks,
such as when you're

00:26:42.770 --> 00:26:46.107
asking someone to sign in or use
the product for the first time.

00:26:46.107 --> 00:26:47.940
They're not likely to
go through that again,

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:51.590
but you've got to get
it right the first time.

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:55.190
In my example, our journey
was as a first time user,

00:26:55.190 --> 00:26:58.190
I want to join my next
meeting right now.

00:26:58.190 --> 00:27:01.130
This journey forced us to take
a step back and think about what

00:27:01.130 --> 00:27:04.550
it's like to experience our
product for the first time,

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:07.010
and acknowledge the
options for joining

00:27:07.010 --> 00:27:10.010
could be a little
overwhelming and confusing.

00:27:10.010 --> 00:27:12.410
Critical user journeys
frequently cross

00:27:12.410 --> 00:27:16.010
product and feature
boundaries at a minimum--

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:17.780
at a minimum level,
you should be

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:20.420
aware of how what
you're working on

00:27:20.420 --> 00:27:22.555
fits into the bigger picture.

00:27:22.555 --> 00:27:23.930
With video meetings,
for example,

00:27:23.930 --> 00:27:27.710
we had to think about how this
integrated into calendaring

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:29.930
and chat systems, so
that people could quickly

00:27:29.930 --> 00:27:37.494
move from chat to video, or move
from a calendar into a video.

00:27:37.494 --> 00:27:39.410
We wanted to make sure
the experience is going

00:27:39.410 --> 00:27:42.380
to be excellent, for our
users, so we spent a full week

00:27:42.380 --> 00:27:44.600
going through our
critical user journeys

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:48.200
and doing an audit to document
the potential pitfalls.

00:27:48.200 --> 00:27:52.340
We scrutinized 30 journeys
just like this one,

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:54.890
and we looked at them
across all devices.

00:27:54.890 --> 00:27:57.232
We looked at the iOS
and Android apps.

00:27:57.232 --> 00:27:59.690
We looked at the TV experience,
when you're in a conference

00:27:59.690 --> 00:28:02.210
room, we looked at the web.

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:06.180
We also looked at it through
a number of different lenses.

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:08.600
We had people looking
at the visual design

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:13.760
aspect, the interaction design,
the motion, the audio cues.

00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:15.740
And accessibility.

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:16.422
And writing.

00:28:16.422 --> 00:28:17.880
We even had our
writer take a look.

00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:20.270
So every single person
was doing a pass

00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:23.510
to make sure that
aspect of the experience

00:28:23.510 --> 00:28:25.940
was the best that it could be.

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:29.750
We filed bugs even if a visual
design was a pixel or two off.

00:28:29.750 --> 00:28:31.920
We were pretty strict.

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:34.670
So you can really do critical
user journeys at any time

00:28:34.670 --> 00:28:36.332
during the design process.

00:28:36.332 --> 00:28:37.790
Ideally, you want
to actually start

00:28:37.790 --> 00:28:40.490
thinking about what those
critical user journeys are

00:28:40.490 --> 00:28:43.940
early, so that you're part
of-- maybe when you're

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:46.680
doing your user
stories and scenarios.

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:49.340
But in our case, we had defined
them in the planning phase,

00:28:49.340 --> 00:28:51.470
but we did the audit in
the detailed design phase,

00:28:51.470 --> 00:28:54.800
before we were ready to
start going to launch.

00:28:57.730 --> 00:29:00.050
And then finally
the build phase.

00:29:00.050 --> 00:29:01.690
This is where we
implement, test,

00:29:01.690 --> 00:29:05.530
and evaluate the solution.

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:09.610
The goals of the build phase are
about pride in craftsmanship.

00:29:09.610 --> 00:29:11.840
Attention to detail.

00:29:11.840 --> 00:29:13.820
Testing and launching.

00:29:13.820 --> 00:29:17.692
And assessing the implementation
once it's launched.

00:29:17.692 --> 00:29:19.400
As for the methods,
you want to make sure

00:29:19.400 --> 00:29:21.274
that you've instrumented
the site using tools

00:29:21.274 --> 00:29:24.140
like Google Analytics, or
whatever your preferred one is,

00:29:24.140 --> 00:29:25.880
for performance and
usability metrics.

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:28.670
Remember, when we talked about
the components of usability

00:29:28.670 --> 00:29:31.100
in the beginning, you
want to be confident

00:29:31.100 --> 00:29:34.820
that you're capturing data
like number of clicks, errors,

00:29:34.820 --> 00:29:36.740
and more.

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:39.410
You also want to run
experiments, such as A/B tests,

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:42.751
to see which design is
performing better for users.

00:29:42.751 --> 00:29:44.750
But most importantly, you
want to engage in what

00:29:44.750 --> 00:29:47.720
we call data informed design.

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:49.839
Not data driven design.

00:29:49.839 --> 00:29:51.380
When you are data
driven, you're only

00:29:51.380 --> 00:29:55.880
following the quantitative
results of your research.

00:29:55.880 --> 00:29:58.760
The famous example at
Google is the 41 shades

00:29:58.760 --> 00:30:01.280
of blue, when we
tested, literally,

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:03.800
41 shades of blue links
on the search page

00:30:03.800 --> 00:30:05.780
to see which one
performed better.

00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:08.240
You can look it up,
I'm not kidding.

00:30:08.240 --> 00:30:10.340
And, that's great.

00:30:10.340 --> 00:30:13.584
We saw actual,
different results when

00:30:13.584 --> 00:30:15.500
we looked at all the
different shades of blue.

00:30:15.500 --> 00:30:19.010
But that's a bit soulless.

00:30:19.010 --> 00:30:22.700
It's not the way that we want
to think about using data

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:25.910
in our design process.

00:30:25.910 --> 00:30:28.340
When you're data driven,
you only follow--

00:30:28.340 --> 00:30:30.340
you're only looking
at the hard data,

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:32.630
but that data can
sometimes be misleading.

00:30:32.630 --> 00:30:36.470
Because it will tell you
the what, but not the why.

00:30:36.470 --> 00:30:38.270
And understanding
the why is where

00:30:38.270 --> 00:30:41.090
you are able to take action.

00:30:41.090 --> 00:30:42.830
Instead, use the
data and combine it

00:30:42.830 --> 00:30:47.510
with qualitative feedback to
assess the complete picture.

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:50.246
This is data informed design.

00:30:50.246 --> 00:30:51.620
Quantitative
metrics are also not

00:30:51.620 --> 00:30:55.220
often useful for evaluating
the impact of the UX changes

00:30:55.220 --> 00:30:56.210
that you make.

00:30:56.210 --> 00:30:58.130
This is because
they're pretty general.

00:30:58.130 --> 00:30:59.930
They don't usually
directly relate

00:30:59.930 --> 00:31:01.850
to either the quality
of the user experience

00:31:01.850 --> 00:31:04.100
or the goals of your
product, so it's

00:31:04.100 --> 00:31:06.140
hard to make them actionable.

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:08.870
So to help with this, a few
smart researchers at Google

00:31:08.870 --> 00:31:11.420
came up with a method
that we called HEART.

00:31:15.430 --> 00:31:18.560
The HEART framework is
useful for evaluating

00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:20.450
the quality of the
user experience

00:31:20.450 --> 00:31:24.040
and the impact of UX
changes over time.

00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:27.130
Happiness-- measures
user attitudes.

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:31.390
Often collected via those
surveys we talked about.

00:31:31.390 --> 00:31:36.100
For example, satisfaction
surveys, perceived ease of use,

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:41.080
Net Promoter Score
is a good example.

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:44.830
Engagement-- measures the
level of user involvement.

00:31:44.830 --> 00:31:47.170
Typically via
behavioral proxies,

00:31:47.170 --> 00:31:50.500
such as frequency, intensity,
or depth of interaction

00:31:50.500 --> 00:31:53.560
over some time period.

00:31:53.560 --> 00:31:56.740
Examples might include the
number of visits per user

00:31:56.740 --> 00:31:58.450
per week, or the
number of photos

00:31:58.450 --> 00:32:01.154
uploaded per user per day.

00:32:01.154 --> 00:32:03.070
For Hangouts, we want
to know how often people

00:32:03.070 --> 00:32:05.528
are creating video meetings
and maybe how long has meetings

00:32:05.528 --> 00:32:07.870
are.

00:32:07.870 --> 00:32:12.220
Adoption measures the number
of new users of a product

00:32:12.220 --> 00:32:13.720
or feature.

00:32:13.720 --> 00:32:15.310
For example, you
want to-- in this one

00:32:15.310 --> 00:32:18.250
you want to look at
the number of new users

00:32:18.250 --> 00:32:19.375
that are creating accounts.

00:32:21.980 --> 00:32:23.680
Maybe over a period
of time, like

00:32:23.680 --> 00:32:25.660
accounts created
in last seven days,

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:31.300
or the percentage of new users
who create video meetings.

00:32:31.300 --> 00:32:34.150
Retention is the rate
at which existing users

00:32:34.150 --> 00:32:37.030
are returning to your product.

00:32:37.030 --> 00:32:40.600
For example, how many of the
active users from a given time

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:43.390
period are still present
sometime later in the chat,

00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:44.660
in the same time period?

00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:47.620
You might be more interested
in failure to retain,

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:52.250
which is what we commonly known
as-- commonly known as churn.

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:55.760
And then Task Success includes
the traditional behavioral

00:32:55.760 --> 00:32:58.250
metrics.

00:32:58.250 --> 00:33:01.340
These include efficiency,
the time to complete a task,

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:05.490
effectiveness, the percent
of tasks completed.

00:33:05.490 --> 00:33:07.460
And the error rate that
we discussed earlier.

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:10.600
This category is most applicable
to areas of your product

00:33:10.600 --> 00:33:13.820
where they're very task
focused, such as doing a search

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:16.370
or upload flow.

00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:19.100
So we're often asked why measure
adoption retention when you

00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:21.590
can just count unique users?

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:23.900
It's definitely important
to count how many users you

00:33:23.900 --> 00:33:25.500
had in a given time period.

00:33:25.500 --> 00:33:27.610
For example, 70 actives.

00:33:27.610 --> 00:33:30.110
But if you measure
adoption retention as well,

00:33:30.110 --> 00:33:32.450
you're going to be explicitly
distinguishing new users

00:33:32.450 --> 00:33:33.999
from returning users.

00:33:33.999 --> 00:33:35.540
So you can tell how
quickly your user

00:33:35.540 --> 00:33:39.200
base is growing or stabilizing.

00:33:39.200 --> 00:33:41.510
This is especially useful
for new products and features

00:33:41.510 --> 00:33:44.679
or those being redesigned.

00:33:44.679 --> 00:33:46.720
Now, you don't necessarily
need to create metrics

00:33:46.720 --> 00:33:47.757
in all these categories.

00:33:47.757 --> 00:33:49.840
You should choose the ones
that are most important

00:33:49.840 --> 00:33:51.640
for your particular project.

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:54.190
However, no matter what
type of metric you choose,

00:33:54.190 --> 00:33:56.740
there's one important
principle that you always

00:33:56.740 --> 00:33:58.210
want to stick to.

00:33:58.210 --> 00:34:00.820
You want to ensure
that the metrics are

00:34:00.820 --> 00:34:03.535
in line with the user goals that
you defined for your product.

00:34:07.830 --> 00:34:11.810
So to wrap up, usability
is about keeping the user

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:15.050
at the center of your
product development process.

00:34:15.050 --> 00:34:19.219
You want to consider the
learnability, the efficiency,

00:34:19.219 --> 00:34:23.270
the memorability, the
accessibility, errors,

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:26.960
and satisfaction
inherent in your product.

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:28.460
I shared some of
the methods that we

00:34:28.460 --> 00:34:32.960
use a Google, like experience
mapping and HEART metrics,

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:35.389
which I hope you found
useful and you can obviously

00:34:35.389 --> 00:34:38.210
try in your own organizations.

00:34:38.210 --> 00:34:41.150
We've touched at
very high levels

00:34:41.150 --> 00:34:43.880
on some of these
methods, and so I

00:34:43.880 --> 00:34:48.980
encourage you to look and
find resources on your own

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:50.124
in the web.

00:34:50.124 --> 00:34:52.040
These are some of these
are fairly well known,

00:34:52.040 --> 00:34:56.600
some of these are more
inherent to Google's culture.

00:34:56.600 --> 00:35:00.830
But I really just hope that
if you learned one thing here

00:35:00.830 --> 00:35:05.120
today, it's how important it is
to involve the user when you're

00:35:05.120 --> 00:35:08.630
building products if you want
them to use them, and trust

00:35:08.630 --> 00:35:09.570
them, and love them.

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:15.500
And since you're
all Google's users,

00:35:15.500 --> 00:35:18.040
you can also be a part
of our design process.

00:35:18.040 --> 00:35:19.300
So get involved.

00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:22.840
If you're a GSuite administrator
or an end user of our tools,

00:35:22.840 --> 00:35:27.740
you can sign up to be a part
of our GSuite user panel.

00:35:27.740 --> 00:35:29.890
If you're a developer or
a cloud platform user,

00:35:29.890 --> 00:35:33.864
you can sign up as well for our
Google cloud platform panel.

00:35:33.864 --> 00:35:35.530
I'll leave this up
for a bit if you guys

00:35:35.530 --> 00:35:37.165
want to take a snapshot.

00:35:39.910 --> 00:35:42.850
I also want to use this time
to promote a session coming up

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:43.720
in the next room.

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:45.460
I think it's room number eight.

00:35:45.460 --> 00:35:47.320
It's coming up right after this.

00:35:47.320 --> 00:35:50.570
It's titled Driving Product
Excellence With User Feedback,

00:35:50.570 --> 00:35:52.180
which will go deeper
into our research

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:55.850
practices and our trusted tester
and early adopter programs.

00:35:55.850 --> 00:35:58.810
So if you want to learn more
about that, and the activities

00:35:58.810 --> 00:36:01.510
that we do around
actually going on site

00:36:01.510 --> 00:36:03.640
and talking to our customers.

00:36:03.640 --> 00:36:06.279
That's a great session to go to.

00:36:06.279 --> 00:36:08.320
Late last year we used
our trusted tester program

00:36:08.320 --> 00:36:10.150
to validate key features
of our products,

00:36:10.150 --> 00:36:11.920
such as supporting
larger meeting sizes,

00:36:11.920 --> 00:36:15.370
and making it easier to join
meetings by phone or video.

00:36:15.370 --> 00:36:17.140
These weren't just UX issues.

00:36:17.140 --> 00:36:19.120
They were essential for
us to know whether we

00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:21.590
had a product market fit.

00:36:21.590 --> 00:36:23.830
We also invited several
particularly engaged

00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:26.290
large customers to have
a seat at the table

00:36:26.290 --> 00:36:28.390
during the design process.

00:36:28.390 --> 00:36:30.220
We visited some of
those testers to watch

00:36:30.220 --> 00:36:32.080
how the product
was used in action,

00:36:32.080 --> 00:36:36.430
providing valuable insights
to improve our usability.

00:36:36.430 --> 00:36:38.530
So thank you so much
for coming to Next

00:36:38.530 --> 00:36:40.060
and coming to my session.

00:36:40.060 --> 00:36:43.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

